{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent positive bias: forecasts are close for days 1–7, but become increasingly too high from ~day 8 onward; apply a growing downward adjustment with horizon (small in week 1, moderate in week 2, large in weeks 3–4).",
    "Trend/mean-reversion miss in selloffs: when recent history shows a sharp downtrend, the model predicts stabilization/rebound too early; enforce stronger continuation of the prevailing downtrend for the next ~10–20 days before allowing mean reversion.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: after a short-term uptick in history, the model extrapolates a sustained rally (often back toward mid-70s) that does not materialize; cap rebound strength and fade upward slopes beyond ~5–10 days.",
    "Excessive smoothness / lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts (bearish break) and keeps levels anchored near prior averages; add a regime-switch penalty that pulls forecasts toward the most recent 5–10 day level rather than the 30-day mean.",
    "Terminal-level error: end-of-horizon forecasts drift upward even when true outcomes stay flat-to-lower; impose an end-horizon anchor so day-30 level cannot exceed (recent spot + small buffer) unless recent momentum is strongly positive."
  ]
}
